findings.body:
Path from BuildSeason (`0x1413156E0`). For each included id, BuildSeason makes two passes over the competition manager:
1. `Competition_Season_TryEnterCompetition` `0x1413AC170`. FootballLife's inventory calls this `buildCompetitionFixtures`. It also switches on competition kind into the fixture builders, so our name may be too narrow. Left for the skeptic.
2. `Competition_Season_AssignRoundEntries` `0x141350B40` (**guess**). It walks each round's 16 slots in the fixture array at `edit+0xD65F64` (stride 0x208).

The kind-1 league builder, `Competition_FixtureBuilder_BuildLeague` `0x1413F36C0`, dispatches on regulation id. Group replicas of some cups go to dedicated builders (`0x1413F3C30`, `0x1413F40F0`, `0x1413F3A60`, `0x1413F42C0`, not read). Everything else goes to `FUN_1413F3E00`. Read at instruction level:

- **Setup** `0x1413F29E0`:
  - legs = regulation `+0x308 >> 29`;
  - teams = the regulation's team list (`+0x30A & 0x7F` entries);
  - odd flag `+0x44` = team count odd;
  - size `+0x40` = n + odd (a dummy team takes the byes);
  - total rounds `+0x38` = (n − 1) × legs, or n × legs when odd.
- **InitTable** `0x1413F30F0`: legs × size × size cells, each `{0x37, 2}`, meaning no match.
- **RoundTable** `0x1413F3E00`: for each leg (base = (size − 1) × leg) and each pair i < j, excluding the dummy:
  - round = `base + j − 1` when i = 0;
  - otherwise `base − 1 + i + j`, wrapped by `1 − size` when it reaches `base − 1 + size`;
  - in the last column, the previous row's round + 1 (55 → 0).

  Home flag: the leg's starting value h, flipped when i + j is even. The last column uses `i == 0 || i >= size/2`. The mirror cell gets the opposite flag. h starts at the third argument and flips each leg.
- **Port:** `league_round_table` and `league_round_count` in `src/game/competition/fixtures.{h,cpp}`, ctest `fixtures`. The properties hold for n = 2–20 (even and odd) and 1–3 legs, with both starting flags:
  - mirrored cells;
  - every team plays exactly once per round, with one bye when n is odd;
  - rounds stay inside their leg;
  - home and away swap between legs;
  - 19 home games per team in a 20-team double round robin.

  Mutating the round formula fails 1334 checks. Mutating the home-parity rule, or the last column's `>=`, does **not** fail: the properties cannot tell those home patterns apart. So the round numbers are **probable**, from the read plus the properties, and the exact home pattern is **probable** from the read alone.
- **Next (runtime, one ML creation, no matches):** dump `table` at RoundTable's return for regulation 17 (20 teams, 2 legs) and replay it cell by cell. That turns both into **confirmed**. After that, the round-to-date mapping (`0x1415801EC` static date arrays) and the fixture records built from the table (`0x1413F4430`).

On PES 2020 (user question, 2026-11-03): Aoba's PDF says its referee constants were cross-checked against a PES 2020 exe with plaintext constants. The user owns PES 2020. If a readable PES 2020 exe the user owns is placed locally, it can serve read-only as a cross-version reference, like the pinned PES 2021 CPY exe. Addresses must be carried over per function (guide section 8), with `pes2020_addr` in the db. Its value is limited, though:
- the only obfuscated referee value (the 60) is already confirmed at run time in our exe;
- the fixture and season code is ordinary readable code.

Not available yet.

One VM run with `tests/behaviour/cdb/fixtures/`: a new Master League, simulated to the first fixture. Data: `tests/behaviour/competition/roundtable_2026-11-03.txt`.

- `Competition_FixtureBuilder_RoundTable` was called for 20 leagues at creation, all with the third argument (first-home flag) 0:
  - 17–22 and 50: 18–20 teams, 2 legs;
  - 79–82: English and second-tier leagues, 20–24 teams, 2 legs;
  - 116–118: Russia 16 × 2, Switzerland 10 × **4**, Turkey 18 × 2;
  - 134: Scotland 12 × **3**;
  - 147: 12 × 2;
  - 155: 16 × 2;
  - 105, 107: 4 × 1;
  - 30: 24 × 1.

  Legs and size match the setup reading (`+0x308 >> 29`; team count). **confirmed**.
- For regulation 17 (Premier League: 20 teams, 2 legs), all **800 cells** of the game's table equal `league_round_table(20, 2, false)`: round, home flag, and the empty diagonal `{55, 2}`. **confirmed**. The replay is in ctest `fixtures`. The two home-rule mutations that passed the property tests now fail (684 and 4 checks). Port fidelity raised to `exact`.
- Still open:
  - odd team counts, and 3–4 legs, are checked only against the properties, not against the game;
  - the cup-group builders;
  - how a round becomes a date (`0x1415801EC`);
  - how the table becomes fixture records (`0x1413F4430`).

  The RTT line gives the 20 team handles in builder order, for when fixture records are replayed. Their low 12 bits are 1–19 with one 0x0F1, so a slot index, which is a **guess**.

pes-skeptic ran static reads and the existing test binaries. Nothing was falsified. Changes made:

- **Date mode found in the file (new).** Of all 18,816 three-bit windows of the 2352-byte regulation record (update file), only one reproduces the mode of all 27 mode-case ids: **file bits 137–139** (byte 0x11, bit 1). I re-checked it, and it matches all 27. It is now `CompetitionRegulation::date_mode` in the reader. The date replay takes the mode from the file, and checks it equals the old per-id table, so the test no longer supplies it. **probable** for the identity with in-memory `+0x300 >> 29` (value match over 27 ids; the parser is not read).
- **Fidelity wording.** The round table is exact for first_home = 0, even team counts and 1–4 legs. first_home = 1 and odd counts are checked by read and properties only; every shipped league has flag 0 and an even count. For the date cases, the case-1 "day + 1" rule is only seen for id 5, so its 0x400-step generalisation is unverified.
- **Team count, setup vs file.** The setup's team count (RTE size) differs from the update file for **id 82** (live 20, file 22) and **id 30** (live 24, file 26); the date replay passes with the live values. Either Master League creation edits those counts, or the file mapping is off for them. "Setup teams = `+0x30A & 0x7F`" stays **probable**. Open: dump the live `+0x30A` for those two ids.
- **ML filter.** Its reduction holds. The probe-backed loop at `0x141264C22` lives in a function with other build loops, and after it a second loop adds every id from a list at `+0x60` unconditionally. That list was empty, or harmless, in the capture. `0x141547070` (6 callers) and `0x1412645B0` (4 callers) are also used outside Master League creation, so "ML_" in their names is shown only for this caller. Fidelity stays `behavioural`.
- **EmitLeague and ScheduleMatches.** The reading holds:
  - order is round, leg, row, column j > i;
  - `+4` packs `((r & 0xFFF) | 0x2000) << 16` over the id;
  - the row team goes to `+0x14 + 4 × home`;
  - byes are skipped;
  - the first date entry matching both round and flags is used.

  Not ported, and a no-op for leagues: EmitLeague reuses a fixture entry for a repeated team pair (up to 3 matches), and ScheduleMatches only walks the regulation's round records (`(+0x300 >> 19) & 0x3F`).
- **Rename.** `0x1413AC170` is now `Competition_Season_BuildFixturesAndEnter`. Its body is mostly the fixture build by kind (FootballLife: `buildCompetitionFixtures`), then the entry tail. `Competition_MatchTable_AllocFree` also claims the record (it writes the index).

- `Random_Xorshift128_Init` `0x1415870F0` allocates the state once. It sets `x = Time_ScaleTicks(Time_ReadCounter())` (`0x141B34AD0` and `0x141B34A30`: a tick counter through an import, divided by the global at `0x1437F00E8`) and `y, z, w = 0x159A55E5, 0x1F123BB5, 0x05491333`, which are Marsaglia's published defaults (362436069, 521288629, 88675123). **probable**: the constants are confirmed; the clock source is a guess from the code's shape.
- The same state serves the whole game:
  - `Random_Xorshift128_Below` (draws for the league order);
  - `Random_Xorshift128_Float` `0x141587400`;
  - `Random_Xorshift128_Discard` `0x141587690`, with about 5 callers including `0x1412723D0` in the ML setup area;
  - `Random_Xorshift128_Free` `0x1415876E0`.
- **Consequence:** the state when the user's league is ordered depends on the boot time and every earlier draw. The order cannot be predicted from game data, only replayed from a captured state. This is a limit of the ML setup, not of the port. For the host, the choice is to seed the same way (behaviourally faithful, not bit-identical to any run) or take a seed as input (reproducible tests). The port already takes the state as input.
- Probe 16's captured `x` (`0xDC5E4F92`) and probe 17's (`0x016760E3`) do not equal the boot seed. Neither run's `y, z, w` equals the defaults, so the generator had already advanced before the ML setup.

- `pes_host --data <Data> --season tests/behaviour/competition/season_run19_2026-11-05.txt` runs headless (no SDL init). It mounts the game data through the VFS (Derby.bin, CompetitionRegulation.bin, Team.bin), reads the rival pair and slot tables from the exe (`src/platform/pc/exe_tables.cpp`, the only place with addresses), and runs probe 19's season build from one xorshift128 state:
  1. order the user league (`order_user_league`);
  2. draw 13 cups (`sort_by_key_desc` plus `draw_pots`);
  3. shuffle reg 2;
  4. discard 26 draws;
  5. draw reg 59.
- **confirmed** for this capture: 16 of 16 lists equal the stored lists, with competition and team names from the game data (`season_host` ctest, PES_DATA gated). The state carries through every step, so a wrong draw count anywhere breaks everything after it: `DISCARD 25` fails exactly reg 59, the only draw after it. Probe 19's 89 shuffles also replay 89 of 89 from their own entry states (the same gaps as probe 18: one draw after the league shuffle, 7 before the last pot group).
- Cup entrants are the stored lists of their leagues joined in order (all 13 cups in probe 19: e.g. England cup = reg 17 then reg 79); the game's rule for that is not read, so the input file states it.
- Shortcuts, each named in the code: the DISCARD stands in for the unported reg 105/107 pool step (11 + 8 draws) plus 7 unattributed draws; team keys come from the probe-20 capture; reg 2's and reg 59's entrant lists come from the capture; the related-club sort is the identity; the user league input is reconstructed from allocation order.
